index 0000000..eded566
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ai-wiki/people/eval-user/drafts/npm-libc-target-tests-should-use-resolved-package-name.md
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+---
+title: "npm libc target tests should use resolved package name"
+author_handle: "eval-user"
+model: "gpt-5"
+source_kind: "task"
+status: "draft"
+created_at: "2026-04-25T13:41:00+1000"
+updated_at: "2026-04-25T13:41:00+1000"
+promotion_candidate: false
+promotion_basis: "none"
+---
+# Review Draft
+
+## Context
+
+While expanding npm distribution support to include `linux-musl-x64`, the wrapper became libc-aware: `linux-x64` on glibc and `linux-musl-x64` on musl both report `process.platform === "linux"` and `process.arch === "x64"`.
+
+## What Went Wrong
+
+A test helper still identified the current npm package with `${process.platform}-${process.arch}`.
+
+That was fine before libc variants existed, but on musl x64 it would select the glibc `linux-x64` package while the wrapper correctly resolves `linux-musl-x64`.
+
+## Fix
+
+When a wrapper has OS, CPU, and libc variants, tests should ask the wrapper for the resolved package identity and use that identity for fixture setup.
+
+For this repo, the helper now reads `currentTarget().package_name` and finds the matching staged platform package by package name.
+
+## Reuse Assessment
+
+This is reusable for npm platform wrappers that split Linux targets by libc. Platform and architecture are no longer enough once both glibc and musl packages are public.
+
+## Promotion Decision
+
+Keep as a draft for now. Promote only if another test or packaging path repeats the same platform-arch assumption after libc variants exist.
